          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Chemistry is the branch of physical science that studies the properties of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+matter"} condensed matter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atom+%28physics%29"} atoms
            upwards.
          %p
            A central part of chemistry is the identification of reaction processes –
            %em chemical reactions
            – by which a variety of chemical substances (species of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atom+%28physics%29"} atoms
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/molecules"} molecules
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mi B
                  %mo ↔
                  %mi c
                  %mi C
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a A + b B \leftrightarrow c C + d D
          %p
            where the capital letters denote species of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atom+%28physics%29"} atoms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/molecules"} molecules
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            molecules of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            may react with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            molecules of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            molecules of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            together with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            molecules of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamic+equilibrium"} thermodynamic equilibrium
            such reactions occur in both directions, whence the double arrow. A reaction of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                  %mi C
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \to c C + d D
          %p
            would be called a
            %em decay
            process. Notice that analogous processes appear elsewhere, for instance there are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nuclear+reactions"} nuclear reactions
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
            described by the same kind of reaction diagrams. Also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"} topological string
            undergo reaction processes of this form, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#TriangulatedCategoryWithStability"} below
          %p
            One might be tempted to recognize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            in this notation, but care needs to be exercised to arrive at a sensible concept.
          %h2#formalization_of_reaction_processes Formalization of reaction processes
          %p
            It is unlikely that any formalization of reaction processes (say in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chemical+elements"} chemical elements
            %em stochastic Petri nets
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %em triangulated categories with stability conditions
          %h3#stochastic_petri_nets_or_chemical_reaction_networks Stochastic Petri nets or Chemical reaction networks
          %p
            Given a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            of ‘species’, a
            %strong complex
            of those species is a function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C : S \to \mathbb{N}
            %strong reaction network
            is a set of species together with a directed multigraph whose vertices are labelled by complexes of those species. Edges of the multigraph correspond to transitions.
          %p
            Any reaction network gives rise to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Petri+net"} Petri net
            = succeed "." do
              %em vice versa
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0, \infty)
            %strong stochastic
            versions of the nets and networks. (See
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#BaezNet"} BaezNet
          %h3#TriangulatedCategoryWithStability Triangulated categories with stability conditions
          %p
            One successful formalization in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            of the idea of reaction processes appears in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"} topological string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            (of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
            on some
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+variety"} Calabi-Yau variety
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↔
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \leftrightarrow B \oplus C
          %p
            by which a brane of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            may decay into a brane of type B and a brane of type C.
          %p
            While these are not reactions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chemical+elements"} chemical elements
          %p
            And for the case of these D-branes, there happens to be a well-established and useful mathematical formalization of these reaction processes via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p
            Namely every reaction process as above corresponds to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+sequence"} homotopy fiber sequence
            (a “distinguished triangle”) in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  B \longrightarrow A \longrightarrow C
                  \\,.
          %p
            (See
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Aspinwall04"} Aspinwall 04
          %p
            Mathematically such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+sequence"} homotopy fiber sequence
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            precisely expresses the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a “twisted
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension"}> extension
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semidirect+product"} semidirect product
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
          %p
            In addition there are
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bridgeland+stability+conditions"} Bridgeland stability conditions
            on such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"} triangulated categories
            of topological D-branes. These determine which of these reaction processes lead to stable compounds, i.e. whether, in the above example, the brane of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            will really decay into branes of type B and C, or if conversely the latter will fuse. (See again
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Aspinwall04"} Aspinwall 04
          %p
            After its motivation from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            reaction processes, the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"} triangulated categories
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bridgeland+stability+conditions"} Bridgeland stability conditions
            has become a rich and active area of pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            in itself.
